('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',1,null,$q$If 5x − 3 = 2x + 12, what is the value of x?$q$,$j$["3","5","9","15"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Subtract 2x from both sides: 3x − 3 = 12. Add 3: 3x = 15. Divide by 3: x = 5.$q$,$j${"3":"15 is the value of 3x. Divide by 3 to finish."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',1,null,$q$A plumber charges a $60 service fee plus $45 per hour of work. Which equation gives the total charge C, in dollars, for a job that takes h hours?$q$,$j$["C = 45h + 60","C = 45(h + 60)","C = 105h","C = 60h + 45"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$The hourly rate multiplies the number of hours (45h), and the one-time fee is added once (+60).$q$,$j${"2":"This charges the service fee every hour.","3":"This swaps the hourly rate and the one-time fee."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',1,null,$q$What is the slope of the line with equation 4x − 2y = 10?$q$,$j$["−5","−2","2","4"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$Solve for y: −2y = −4x + 10, so y = 2x − 5. The slope is 2.$q$,$j${"0":"−5 is the y-intercept, not the slope.","1":"Check the sign: dividing −4x by −2 gives +2x."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000004','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',1,null,$q$Which ordered pair (x, y) satisfies both y = 3x and x + y = 16?$q$,$j$["(8, 8)","(3, 9)","(12, 4)","(4, 12)"]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$Substitute y = 3x into x + y = 16: 4x = 16, so x = 4 and y = 12.$q$,$j${"2":"The coordinates are reversed; y must be three times x."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',1,null,$q$If 3(x + 4) = 27, what is the value of x + 4?$q$,$j$["5","9","23","27"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Divide both sides by 3: x + 4 = 9. There's no need to solve for x first.$q$,$j${"0":"5 is the value of x, but the question asks for x + 4."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',1,null,$q$A line in the xy-plane has a slope of 4 and passes through the point (0, −3). Which equation defines the line?$q$,$j$["y = −3x + 4","y = 4x + 3","y = 4x − 3","y = 3x − 4"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$Slope-intercept form is y = mx + b with m = 4 and b = −3, so y = 4x − 3.$q$,$j${"0":"This swaps the slope and the y-intercept."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',1,null,$q$If x/3 + 2 = 7, what is the value of x?$q$,$j$["3","5","15","27"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$Subtract 2: x/3 = 5. Multiply by 3: x = 15.$q$,$j${"1":"5 is the value of x/3."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000008','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',2,null,$q$Plan A for a phone costs $35 per month. Plan B costs $20 per month plus a one-time $90 activation fee. After how many months will the total amount paid be the same for both plans?$q$,$j$["3","4","6","9"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$Set the totals equal: 35m = 20m + 90. Then 15m = 90, so m = 6.$q$,$j${"3":"9 comes from dividing 90 by the wrong difference."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000009','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',2,null,$q$Which inequality describes all values of x for which 7 − 2x > 1?$q$,$j$["x < 3","x > −4","x < −3","x > 3"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$Subtract 7: −2x > −6. Dividing by −2 reverses the inequality: x < 3.$q$,$j${"3":"When dividing both sides by a negative number, the inequality sign must flip."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',2,null,$q$Line ℓ is perpendicular to the line y = −(2/5)x + 1 and passes through the point (0, 4). Which equation defines line ℓ?$q$,$j$["y = −(2/5)x + 4","y = −(5/2)x + 4","y = (2/5)x + 4","y = (5/2)x + 4"]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$Perpendicular slopes are negative reciprocals. The reciprocal of −2/5 with the opposite sign is 5/2, and the y-intercept is 4.$q$,$j${"0":"This line is parallel, not perpendicular.","1":"Take the reciprocal and change the sign."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',2,null,$q$2x + 3y = 18
4x − 3y = 0
What is the value of y in the solution to the system of equations above?$q$,$j$["2","3","4","6"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$Add the equations to eliminate y: 6x = 18, so x = 3. Then 2(3) + 3y = 18, so 3y = 12 and y = 4.$q$,$j${"1":"3 is the value of x."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',2,null,$q$A tank holds 500 liters of water and drains at a constant rate of 12 liters per minute. Which equation gives the volume V, in liters, of water remaining in the tank after t minutes?$q$,$j$["V = 12t − 500","V = 488t","V = 500t − 12","V = 500 − 12t"]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$The tank starts at 500 liters and loses 12 liters each minute, so V = 500 − 12t.$q$,$j${"0":"This would give a negative volume at the start."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',2,null,$q$3x + ky = 6
6x + 8y = 5
In the system of equations above, k is a constant. For what value of k does the system have no solution?$q$,$j$["2","4","8","16"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$A system has no solution when the lines are parallel and distinct. The x-coefficients double from 3 to 6, so the y-coefficients must too: 2k = 8, so k = 4. The constants (6 and 5) aren't proportional, so the lines are distinct.$q$,$j${"2":"With k = 8 the slopes differ, so the lines intersect."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',2,null,$q$A store sells notebooks for $3.00 each and pens for $1.50 each. Maya buys 11 items in total and spends exactly $24.00. How many notebooks does she buy?$q$,$j$["3","5","6","8"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Let n be notebooks and p be pens: n + p = 11 and 3n + 1.5p = 24. Substitute p = 11 − n: 3n + 16.5 − 1.5n = 24, so 1.5n = 7.5 and n = 5.$q$,$j${"2":"6 is the number of pens."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000015','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',2,null,$q$The function f is linear, with f(2) = 7 and f(6) = 19. What is the value of f(10)?$q$,$j$["27","29","31","35"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The slope is (19 − 7)/(6 − 2) = 3. From x = 6 to x = 10 the input increases by 4, so f(10) = 19 + 3(4) = 31.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000016','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',3,null,$q$In the xy-plane, line p has equation y = 3x − 5. Line q is parallel to line p and passes through the point (2, 9). What is the y-intercept of line q?$q$,$j$["−5","1","3","9"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$Parallel lines share the slope 3, so line q is y = 3x + b. Substituting (2, 9): 9 = 6 + b, so b = 3.$q$,$j${"0":"−5 is the y-intercept of line p."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000017','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',3,null,$q$If 2a − b = 7 and a + 2b = 11, what is the value of 3a + b?$q$,$j$["9","14","18","25"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$Add the two equations: (2a − b) + (a + 2b) = 3a + b, and 7 + 11 = 18. No need to solve for a and b separately.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000018','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',3,null,$q$A caterer charges a fixed fee plus a constant cost per guest. A party for 40 guests costs $1,100, and a party for 65 guests costs $1,600. What is the caterer's fixed fee?$q$,$j$["$20","$200","$300","$500"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The extra 25 guests cost $500, so each guest costs $20. For 40 guests that's $800, so the fixed fee is $1,100 − $800 = $300.$q$,$j${"0":"$20 is the cost per guest, not the fixed fee.","3":"$500 is the cost of the extra 25 guests."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000019','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',3,null,$q$4x − 6y = 10
y = (2/3)x + c
In the system above, c is a constant. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of c?$q$,$j$["−5/2","−5/3","5/3","10"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Solve the first equation for y: −6y = −4x + 10, so y = (2/3)x − 5/3. For infinitely many solutions the lines must be identical, so c = −5/3.$q$,$j${"2":"Check the sign when dividing 10 by −6."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000020','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',3,null,$q$What is the sum of all solutions to the equation |2x − 5| = 9?$q$,$j$["−2","5","7","9"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Either 2x − 5 = 9 (so x = 7) or 2x − 5 = −9 (so x = −2). The sum is 7 + (−2) = 5.$q$,$j${"2":"7 is only one of the two solutions."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000021','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',3,null,$q$A delivery van's tank holds 24 gallons of fuel, and the van uses 1 gallon for every 18 miles driven. Starting with a full tank, which inequality gives all distances d, in miles, the van can drive while keeping at least 5 gallons in the tank?$q$,$j$["d ≤ 432","d ≥ 342","d ≤ 342","d ≤ 90"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$Fuel remaining is 24 − d/18. Require 24 − d/18 ≥ 5, so d/18 ≤ 19 and d ≤ 342.$q$,$j${"0":"432 miles would empty the tank completely.","3":"90 miles is the distance 5 gallons would cover."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000022','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-algebra',3,null,$q$In the equation 3(2x − k) = 6x + 12, k is a constant. For what value of k does the equation have infinitely many solutions?$q$,$j$["−12","−4","4","12"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Expand: 6x − 3k = 6x + 12. The x-terms match, so the equation is always true exactly when −3k = 12, which gives k = −4.$q$,$j${"2":"Check the sign: −3k = 12 means k is negative."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000023','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',1,null,$q$If f(x) = 3x² − 2, what is the value of f(−2)?$q$,$j$["−14","10","16","34"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$f(−2) = 3(−2)² − 2 = 3(4) − 2 = 10.$q$,$j${"0":"(−2)² is +4, not −4.","3":"Square only x, not 3x."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000024','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',1,null,$q$Which expression is equivalent to (x + 5)(x − 3)?$q$,$j$["x² + 2x + 15","x² + 2x − 15","x² − 15","x² − 2x − 15"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Multiply each term: x² − 3x + 5x − 15 = x² + 2x − 15.$q$,$j${"2":"This skips the middle terms −3x and 5x."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000025','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',1,null,$q$What are the solutions to x² − 9x + 20 = 0?$q$,$j$["x = 4 and x = 5","x = −4 and x = −5","x = 2 and x = 10","x = −2 and x = −10"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$Factor: (x − 4)(x − 5) = 0, so x = 4 or x = 5.$q$,$j${"1":"The numbers inside the factors have the opposite sign of the solutions."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000026','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',1,null,$q$Which expression is equivalent to x⁴ · x³ ?$q$,$j$["2x⁷","x","x¹²","x⁷"]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$When multiplying powers with the same base, add the exponents: 4 + 3 = 7.$q$,$j${"2":"Multiplying exponents applies to a power raised to a power, like (x⁴)³."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000027','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',1,null,$q$If g(x) = 2ˣ, what is the value of g(5)?$q$,$j$["10","25","32","64"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$g(5) = 2⁵ = 2 · 2 · 2 · 2 · 2 = 32.$q$,$j${"0":"10 is 2 · 5, not 2 to the fifth power."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000028','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',1,null,$q$If x² = 49 and x < 0, what is the value of x?$q$,$j$["−49","−24.5","−7","7"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$x² = 49 means x = 7 or x = −7. Since x < 0, x = −7.$q$,$j${"3":"7 is a solution of x² = 49, but it isn't negative."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000029','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',2,null,$q$A bacteria population doubles every 3 hours. If there are 400 bacteria now, how many will there be 12 hours from now?$q$,$j$["1,600","3,200","6,400","19,200"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$12 hours is 4 doubling periods: 400 · 2⁴ = 400 · 16 = 6,400.$q$,$j${"0":"This multiplies by 4 instead of doubling 4 times.","1":"That's only 3 doublings."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',2,null,$q$The value of a machine is $800 when new and decreases by 15% each year. Which function gives its value V, in dollars, t years after it is new?$q$,$j$["V(t) = 800(0.15)ᵗ","V(t) = 800(0.85)ᵗ","V(t) = 800 − 0.15t","V(t) = 800(1.15)ᵗ"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Losing 15% each year leaves 85% of the previous value, so the growth factor is 0.85.$q$,$j${"0":"This keeps only 15% each year.","3":"1.15 would be a 15% increase."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',2,null,$q$What is the minimum value of the function h(x) = x² − 6x + 13 ?$q$,$j$["−5","3","4","13"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The vertex is at x = −(−6)/2 = 3. Then h(3) = 9 − 18 + 13 = 4.$q$,$j${"1":"3 is the x-coordinate of the vertex, not the minimum value.","3":"13 is the y-intercept."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000032','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',2,null,$q$For x ≠ 0, which expression is equivalent to (6x² + 9x) / (3x) ?$q$,$j$["2x + 3","3x + 3","2x + 9x","2x² + 3x"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$Divide each term by 3x: 6x²/3x = 2x and 9x/3x = 3.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000033','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',2,null,$q$If √(x + 7) = 5, what is the value of x?$q$,$j$["−2","18","25","32"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Square both sides: x + 7 = 25, so x = 18.$q$,$j${"0":"Square both sides before subtracting.","2":"25 is the value of x + 7."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000034','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',2,null,$q$The graph of y = (x − 2)² + 5 is shifted 3 units to the left. Which equation describes the new graph?$q$,$j$["y = (x − 2)² + 8","y = (x − 2)² + 2","y = (x + 1)² + 5","y = (x − 5)² + 5"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$Shifting left by 3 replaces x with x + 3: (x + 3 − 2)² + 5 = (x + 1)² + 5.$q$,$j${"1":"This shifts the graph down, not left.","3":"This shifts the graph to the right."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000035','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',2,null,$q$If f(x) = (x − 4)(x + 1), what is the y-intercept of the graph of y = f(x)?$q$,$j$["−4","−3","1","4"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$The y-intercept is f(0) = (0 − 4)(0 + 1) = −4.$q$,$j${"3":"4 is an x-intercept, not the y-intercept."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000036','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',2,null,$q$Which expression is equivalent to 4x² − 25?$q$,$j$["(2x + 5)²","(2x − 5)(2x + 5)","(2x − 5)²","(4x − 5)(x + 5)"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$4x² − 25 is a difference of squares: (2x)² − 5² = (2x − 5)(2x + 5).$q$,$j${"2":"Expanding (2x − 5)² gives a middle term of −20x."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000037','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',2,null,$q$If 3ˣ⁺¹ = 81, what is the value of x?$q$,$j$["3","4","26","27"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$81 = 3⁴, so x + 1 = 4 and x = 3.$q$,$j${"1":"4 is the value of x + 1."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000038','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',3,null,$q$The equation x² + bx + 16 = 0, where b is a positive constant, has exactly one real solution. What is the value of b?$q$,$j$["4","8","16","32"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Exactly one solution means the discriminant is zero: b² − 4(1)(16) = 0, so b² = 64 and b = 8.$q$,$j${"0":"4 is the magnitude of the solution, not b."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000039','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',3,null,$q$Which of the following is a solution to x² − 4x − 3 = 0?$q$,$j$["−2 + √7","2 + √19","2 + √7","4 + √7"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$By the quadratic formula, x = (4 ± √(16 + 12))/2 = (4 ± 2√7)/2 = 2 ± √7.$q$,$j${"3":"Divide the whole numerator by 2, including the 4."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000040','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',3,null,$q$The function f is defined by f(x) = ax² + 4x + c, where a and c are constants. The graph of y = f(x) passes through (0, −6) and (2, 10). What is the value of a?$q$,$j$["−2","1","2","4"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$f(0) = c = −6. Then f(2) = 4a + 8 − 6 = 10, so 4a = 8 and a = 2.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000041','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',3,null,$q$The height h, in feet, of a ball t seconds after it is thrown is modeled by h(t) = −16t² + 48t + 4. What is the maximum height of the ball, in feet?$q$,$j$["1.5","40","52","76"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$The maximum occurs at t = −48/(2 · −16) = 1.5. Then h(1.5) = −16(2.25) + 72 + 4 = 40.$q$,$j${"0":"1.5 is the time of the maximum height, not the height.","3":"This adds 72 + 4 without subtracting 16(2.25)."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000042','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',3,null,$q$For x > 0, which expression is equivalent to (x^(1/2) · x^(3/2)) / x^(−1) ?$q$,$j$["x³","x²","x⁴","x"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$Add exponents in the numerator: 1/2 + 3/2 = 2. Dividing by x^(−1) subtracts −1: 2 − (−1) = 3.$q$,$j${"1":"x² is only the numerator.","3":"Subtracting a negative exponent adds 1."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000043','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',3,null,$q$The balance of a savings account, in dollars, is modeled by B(t) = 1,200(1.04)ᵗ, where t is the number of years since the account was opened. Which statement is the best interpretation of 1.04?$q$,$j$["The balance increases by $4 each year.","The balance increases by 104% each year.","The balance increases by 4% each year.","The balance increases by $1.04 each year."]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$A growth factor of 1.04 means each year's balance is 104% of the previous year's, which is a 4% increase.$q$,$j${"1":"104% of the previous value is a 4% increase, not a 104% increase."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000044','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-advanced-math',3,null,$q$If (x − 3) is a factor of p(x) = x³ − 2x² + kx − 6, where k is a constant, what is the value of k?$q$,$j$["−3","−1","1","3"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$If x − 3 is a factor, then p(3) = 0: 27 − 18 + 3k − 6 = 0, so 3 + 3k = 0 and k = −1.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000045','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',1,null,$q$A recipe uses 3 cups of flour for every 2 cups of sugar. How many cups of flour are needed if 8 cups of sugar are used?$q$,$j$["6","9","12","16"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The ratio is 3:2. 8 cups of sugar is 4 times 2, so use 4 × 3 = 12 cups of flour.$q$,$j${"3":"16 doubles the sugar instead of applying the 3:2 ratio."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000046','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',1,null,$q$A jacket regularly priced at $80 is on sale for 25% off. What is the sale price?$q$,$j$["$20","$55","$60","$100"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$25% of $80 is $20, so the sale price is $80 − $20 = $60.$q$,$j${"0":"$20 is the discount, not the sale price.","3":"This adds 25% instead of subtracting."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000047','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',1,null,$q$The mean of five numbers is 12. What is the sum of the five numbers?$q$,$j$["2.4","12","17","60"]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$Mean = sum ÷ count, so sum = 12 × 5 = 60.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000048','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',1,null,$q$A car travels 210 miles in 3.5 hours at a constant speed. What is the car's speed, in miles per hour?$q$,$j$["60","70","73.5","735"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$Speed = distance ÷ time = 210 ÷ 3.5 = 60 miles per hour.$q$,$j${"3":"735 multiplies instead of dividing."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000049','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',1,$q$Members of a school club, by grade
Grade: 9 | 10 | 11 | 12
Members: 12 | 18 | 15 | 15$q$,$q$What percent of the club's members are in grade 10?$q$,$j$["18%","25%","30%","60%"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The club has 12 + 18 + 15 + 15 = 60 members. 18 ÷ 60 = 0.30, or 30%.$q$,$j${"0":"18 is the number of grade 10 members, not the percent."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000050','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',1,null,$q$A bag contains 4 red, 6 blue, and 10 green marbles. If one marble is chosen at random, what is the probability that it is blue?$q$,$j$["3/10","1/3","3/7","3/5"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$There are 20 marbles and 6 are blue: 6/20 = 3/10.$q$,$j${"2":"This divides by the number of non-blue marbles only."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000051','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',2,null,$q$The price of a share of stock increased from $40 to $50. By what percent did the price increase?$q$,$j$["10%","20%","25%","125%"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$Percent change = (50 − 40)/40 = 10/40 = 25%.$q$,$j${"0":"10 is the dollar change, not the percent.","1":"20% divides the change by the new price instead of the original."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000052','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',2,null,$q$Data set: 4, 7, 7, 9, 13
If the value 13 is replaced with 23, which statement is true?$q$,$j$["Neither the mean nor the median changes.","The median increases, and the mean stays the same.","Both the mean and the median increase.","The mean increases, and the median stays the same."]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$Increasing the largest value raises the sum, so the mean increases. The middle value is still 7, so the median doesn't change.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000053','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',2,null,$q$In a survey of 200 randomly selected students at a school of 1,500 students, 46 said they prefer online classes. Based on the survey, about how many students at the school prefer online classes?$q$,$j$["46","230","345","1,454"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$46/200 = 23% of the sample. 23% of 1,500 is 345.$q$,$j${"0":"46 is the number in the sample, not the estimate for the whole school."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000054','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',2,null,$q$On a map, 1 inch represents 15 miles. Two towns are 4.2 inches apart on the map. What is the actual distance between the towns, in miles?$q$,$j$["3.6","19.2","60","63"]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$4.2 × 15 = 63 miles.$q$,$j${"1":"This adds 15 instead of multiplying."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000055','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',2,null,$q$After a 20% discount, a shirt costs $24. What was the original price of the shirt?$q$,$j$["$19.20","$28.80","$30","$44"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The sale price is 80% of the original: 0.8p = 24, so p = 30.$q$,$j${"1":"Adding 20% of the sale price doesn't undo a 20% discount of the original price."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000056','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',2,$q$Survey of 120 people
Group: Coffee | Tea | Total
Morning person: 34 | 26 | 60
Night person: 40 | 20 | 60
Total: 74 | 46 | 120$q$,$q$If one of the people who prefer tea is chosen at random, what is the probability that the person is a morning person?$q$,$j$["13/60","23/60","13/30","13/23"]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$Restrict to tea drinkers: 46 people, 26 of them morning people. 26/46 = 13/23.$q$,$j${"2":"13/30 = 26/60 is the probability a morning person prefers tea — the condition is reversed."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000057','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',2,null,$q$One kilometer is approximately 0.62 mile. A runner completes a 15-kilometer race. Approximately how many miles is the race?$q$,$j$["0.04","9.3","14.4","24.2"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$15 × 0.62 = 9.3 miles.$q$,$j${"3":"Dividing by 0.62 converts miles to kilometers."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000058','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',2,null,$q$A scatterplot shows hours studied, x, and quiz score, y, for a class. The line of best fit is y = 4.5x + 52. Which is the best interpretation of 4.5 in this context?$q$,$j$["The average quiz score in the class is 4.5 points.","A student who studies 0 hours is predicted to score 4.5 points.","Every student's score increases by exactly 4.5 points per hour studied.","For each additional hour studied, the predicted quiz score increases by about 4.5 points."]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$4.5 is the slope: the predicted change in score per additional hour. It describes the trend, not an exact rule for every student.$q$,$j${"1":"That describes the y-intercept, 52.","2":"A line of best fit predicts; it doesn't guarantee each student's score."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000059','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',3,null,$q$A researcher randomly assigned 100 adult volunteers to use either a new sleep app or no app for a month. The app group reported significantly better sleep quality. Which conclusion is best supported?$q$,$j$["The app improves sleep quality for all adults.","People who sleep better are more likely to use sleep apps.","There is no relationship between the app and sleep quality.","The app is likely to improve sleep quality for adults similar to the volunteers."]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$Random assignment supports a cause-and-effect conclusion, but because the participants were volunteers (not a random sample of all adults), the result generalizes only to people like them.$q$,$j${"0":"The volunteers weren't randomly sampled from all adults."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000060','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',3,null,$q$Data set A: 10, 20, 30, 40, 50
Data set B: 28, 29, 30, 31, 32
Which statement is true?$q$,$j$["The two data sets have equal standard deviations.","The means are equal, and data set A has the greater standard deviation.","Data set A has the greater mean and the greater standard deviation.","The means are equal, and data set B has the greater standard deviation."]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Both sets have a mean of 30. Set A's values are far more spread out from 30, so its standard deviation is greater.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000061','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',3,null,$q$The price of an item is increased by 20% and then the new price is decreased by 20%. The final price is what percent of the original price?$q$,$j$["80%","96%","100%","104%"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$1.20 × 0.80 = 0.96, so the final price is 96% of the original.$q$,$j${"2":"The 20% decrease is taken from a larger amount, so the changes don't cancel."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000062','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',3,null,$q$A metal has a density of 8.9 grams per cubic centimeter. What is the mass, in kilograms, of a solid cube of the metal with edge length 5 centimeters? (1 kilogram = 1,000 grams)$q$,$j$["0.04","1.11","44.5","1,112.5"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Volume = 5³ = 125 cm³. Mass = 125 × 8.9 = 1,112.5 g ≈ 1.11 kg.$q$,$j${"2":"This uses the edge length instead of the volume.","3":"1,112.5 is the mass in grams."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000063','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',3,null,$q$A poll of 600 randomly selected voters found that 54% support a ballot measure. The margin of error is 4 percentage points. Which conclusion is most appropriate?$q$,$j$["Exactly 54% of all voters support the measure.","It is plausible that between 50% and 58% of all voters support the measure.","The poll proves the measure will pass.","At least 58% of all voters support the measure."]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$The margin of error gives a plausible range for the population: 54% ± 4%, or 50% to 58%.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000064','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',3,null,$q$The mean score of 20 students on a test was 78. When one more student's score was included, the mean became 79. What was that student's score?$q$,$j$["79","80","99","100"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The original total is 20 × 78 = 1,560. The new total is 21 × 79 = 1,659. The added score is 1,659 − 1,560 = 99.$q$,$j${"0":"Adding a score equal to the new mean wouldn't raise the mean that much."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000065','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',3,null,$q$In a group of 50 students, 28 take Spanish, 20 take French, and 9 take both. How many students take neither language?$q$,$j$["2","9","11","21"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$Students taking at least one language: 28 + 20 − 9 = 39. Neither: 50 − 39 = 11.$q$,$j${"0":"This forgets to subtract the 9 students counted twice."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000066','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-psda',3,null,$q$An investment grew from $2,000 to $2,420 over two years, increasing by the same percent each year. What was the annual percent increase?$q$,$j$["10%","10.5%","11%","21%"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$2,000(1 + r)² = 2,420, so (1 + r)² = 1.21 and 1 + r = 1.1. The rate is 10%.$q$,$j${"1":"10.5% treats the growth as simple interest instead of compounding.","3":"21% is the total two-year increase."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000067','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',1,null,$q$A rectangle has a length of 12 and a width of 5. What is the length of a diagonal of the rectangle?$q$,$j$["17","√119","60","13"]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$The diagonal is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs 12 and 5: √(144 + 25) = √169 = 13.$q$,$j${"0":"Add the squares of the sides, not the sides themselves."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000068','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',1,null,$q$A circle has a radius of 6. What is the area of the circle?$q$,$j$["6π","36π","12π","144π"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Area = πr² = π(6²) = 36π.$q$,$j${"2":"12π is the circumference.","3":"This squares the diameter instead of the radius."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000069','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',1,null,$q$Two angles of a triangle measure 47° and 68°. What is the measure of the third angle?$q$,$j$["45°","65°","75°","115°"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$The angles of a triangle sum to 180°: 180 − 47 − 68 = 65.$q$,$j${"3":"115° is the sum of the two given angles."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000070','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',1,null,$q$A rectangular box measures 3 centimeters by 4 centimeters by 10 centimeters. What is its volume, in cubic centimeters?$q$,$j$["17","94","120","164"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$Volume = length × width × height = 3 × 4 × 10 = 120.$q$,$j${"3":"164 is the surface area."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000071','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',1,null,$q$In right triangle ABC, angle C is the right angle, AC = 8, and AB = 10. What is the length of BC?$q$,$j$["2","6","12.8","18"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$AB is the hypotenuse: BC² + 8² = 10², so BC² = 36 and BC = 6.$q$,$j${"2":"12.8 treats AB as a leg rather than the hypotenuse."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000072','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',1,null,$q$Two lines intersect. One of the angles formed measures 112°. What is the measure of an angle adjacent to it?$q$,$j$["22°","68°","112°","248°"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Adjacent angles formed by intersecting lines form a straight line, so they sum to 180°: 180 − 112 = 68.$q$,$j${"2":"112° is the vertical (opposite) angle, not the adjacent one."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000073','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',2,null,$q$In right triangle PQR, angle R is the right angle, PR = 5, and QR = 12. What is the value of sin P?$q$,$j$["5/13","5/12","12/13","12/5"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The hypotenuse PQ = √(25 + 144) = 13. The side opposite angle P is QR = 12, so sin P = 12/13.$q$,$j${"0":"5/13 is cos P.","1":"5/12 uses the two legs; that's a tangent ratio, and it's inverted."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000074','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',2,null,$q$A circle in the xy-plane has equation (x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 49. What are the center and the radius of the circle?$q$,$j$["Center (−3, 2), radius 49","Center (3, −2), radius 7","Center (−3, 2), radius 7","Center (3, −2), radius 49"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$In (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r², the center is (h, k) = (3, −2) and r² = 49, so r = 7.$q$,$j${"2":"The signs inside the parentheses are opposite to the center's coordinates.","3":"49 is r², not r."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000075','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',2,null,$q$Triangle ABC is similar to triangle DEF, with side AB corresponding to side DE and side BC corresponding to side EF. If AB = 4, DE = 10, and BC = 6, what is EF?$q$,$j$["2.4","12","15","24"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The scale factor from ABC to DEF is 10/4 = 2.5, so EF = 6 × 2.5 = 15.$q$,$j${"0":"This scales in the wrong direction.","1":"This adds 6 to BC instead of scaling."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000076','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',2,null,$q$An arc of a circle with radius 9 is intercepted by a central angle of 80°. What is the length of the arc?$q$,$j$["4π","18π","8π","2π"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$Arc length = (80/360) × 2π(9) = (2/9) × 18π = 4π.$q$,$j${"1":"18π is the full circumference."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000077','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',2,null,$q$An equilateral triangle has a perimeter of 18. What is its area?$q$,$j$["9√3","36","18√3","9"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$Each side is 6. Area = (√3/4)s² = (√3/4)(36) = 9√3.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000078','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',2,null,$q$A right circular cylinder has a radius of 3 and a height of 8. What is the volume of the cylinder?$q$,$j$["24π","48π","66π","72π"]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$V = πr²h = π(9)(8) = 72π.$q$,$j${"0":"Square the radius.","2":"66π is the surface area."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000079','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',2,null,$q$In a right triangle, one acute angle measures x°, and sin(x°) = 0.6. What is the value of cos((90 − x)°)?$q$,$j$["0.4","0.6","0.8","1.6"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$The sine of an angle equals the cosine of its complement, so cos((90 − x)°) = sin(x°) = 0.6.$q$,$j${"2":"0.8 is cos(x°), not cos((90 − x)°)."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000080','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',2,null,$q$A 30°-60°-90° triangle has a hypotenuse of length 14. What is the length of the side opposite the 60° angle?$q$,$j$["14√3","7√2","7","7√3"]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$The short leg (opposite 30°) is half the hypotenuse, 7. The long leg (opposite 60°) is 7√3.$q$,$j${"2":"7 is the side opposite the 30° angle."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000081','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',3,null,$q$A circle in the xy-plane has equation x² + y² − 10x + 4y − 7 = 0. What is the radius of the circle?$q$,$j$["6","√7","36","7"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$Complete the square: (x − 5)² + (y + 2)² = 7 + 25 + 4 = 36, so the radius is 6.$q$,$j${"2":"36 is r²."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000082','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',3,null,$q$Points A(1, 2) and B(7, 10) are the endpoints of a diameter of a circle. What is the area of the circle?$q$,$j$["10π","100π","25π","50π"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$AB = √(6² + 8²) = 10, so the radius is 5 and the area is 25π.$q$,$j${"1":"This uses the diameter as the radius."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000083','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',3,null,$q$Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. Two interior angles on the same side of the transversal measure (3x + 10)° and (2x + 20)°. What is the value of x?$q$,$j$["10","30","34","50"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Same-side interior angles are supplementary: 5x + 30 = 180, so x = 30.$q$,$j${"0":"Setting the angles equal applies to alternate interior angles, not same-side ones."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000084','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',3,null,$q$A square is inscribed in a circle of radius 5. What is the area of the square?$q$,$j$["25","100","50","25π"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The square's diagonal is a diameter, 10. A square with diagonal d has area d²/2 = 100/2 = 50.$q$,$j${"1":"100 is the square of the diagonal, not the area."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000085','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',3,null,$q$In triangle ABC, angle B is a right angle and tan A = 3/4. If AC = 20, what is the length of BC?$q$,$j$["9","12","15","16"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$tan A = BC/AB = 3/4, so the sides are in a 3-4-5 ratio with hypotenuse AC = 20 (scale factor 4). BC = 3 × 4 = 12.$q$,$j${"3":"16 is AB, the side adjacent to angle A."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000086','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',3,null,$q$A right circular cone has a volume of 96π cubic centimeters and a height of 8 centimeters. What is the radius of its base, in centimeters?$q$,$j$["6","4","12","2√3"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$V = (1/3)πr²h: (1/3)πr²(8) = 96π, so r² = 36 and r = 6.$q$,$j${"3":"This forgets the 1/3 in the cone volume formula."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000087','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',3,null,$q$An angle measures 5π/6 radians. What is its measure in degrees?$q$,$j$["75","150","216","300"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Multiply by 180/π: (5π/6)(180/π) = 150.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b010000-0000-4000-8000-000000000088','SAT',$q$Math$q$,'sat-geo-trig',3,null,$q$The legs of a right triangle are in the ratio 5:12, and the triangle's perimeter is 60. What is the length of the hypotenuse?$q$,$j$["10","13","24","26"]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$The sides are 5k, 12k, and 13k, so 30k = 60 and k = 2. The hypotenuse is 13(2) = 26.$q$,$j${"1":"13 is the ratio value, before scaling.","2":"24 is the longer leg."}$j$::jsonb,95,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',1,$q$Honeybees communicate the location of food through a "waggle dance." A forager returning to the hive moves in a figure-eight pattern, and the angle of the straight middle run relative to vertical indicates the direction of the food relative to the sun. The duration of that run signals distance: the longer the waggle, the farther away the flowers.$q$,$q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$,$j$["Foragers dance only when the sun is directly overhead.","The figure-eight pattern is used to attract other bees to the dancer.","The waggle dance conveys both the direction of and the distance to a food source.","Honeybees prefer flowers that are far from the hive."]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The text explains that the angle signals direction and the duration signals distance — both parts of the location.$q$,$j${"1":"The text doesn't say the pattern attracts bees; it explains what the pattern encodes.","3":"The text says longer dances mean farther food, not that bees prefer it."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',1,$q$The Great Pacific Garbage Patch is often imagined as a floating island of trash. In reality, most of the debris consists of tiny plastic fragments called microplastics, suspended at or below the surface. Because the particles are so small and scattered, the patch is difficult to see from a ship and cannot be photographed from space.$q$,$q$According to the text, why is the Great Pacific Garbage Patch hard to see?$q$,$j$["It sinks to the ocean floor during the day.","Most of its debris consists of small, scattered particles.","It is located far from any shipping routes.","It is mostly made of transparent glass."]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$The text states that the particles are so small and scattered that the patch is difficult to see.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',1,$q$Sourdough bread rises without commercial yeast. Instead, bakers use a "starter" — a mixture of flour and water that captures wild yeast and bacteria from the environment. The bacteria produce lactic and acetic acids, which give sourdough its characteristic tangy flavor, while the wild yeast produces the gas that makes the dough rise.$q$,$q$Based on the text, what is responsible for the tangy flavor of sourdough bread?$q$,$j$["The type of flour used in the dough","The length of time the bread is baked","Acids produced by bacteria in the starter","Gas produced by commercial yeast"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The text says bacteria produce lactic and acetic acids, which give sourdough its tangy flavor.$q$,$j${"3":"The text says sourdough uses no commercial yeast, and gas causes rising, not flavor."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000004','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',1,$q$Many desert plants open their stomata — tiny pores used for gas exchange — only at night. Taking in carbon dioxide during the cooler nighttime hours allows the plants to keep their stomata closed during the hot day, when water would otherwise evaporate rapidly through the open pores.$q$,$q$According to the text, what is a benefit of opening stomata only at night?$q$,$j$["It increases the plant's growth rate during the day.","It allows the plant to absorb more sunlight.","It protects the plant from nighttime predators.","It reduces the plant's water loss."]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$Keeping stomata closed during the hot day prevents water from evaporating rapidly through them.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',1,$q$Before the invention of the mechanical clock, many towns in medieval Europe kept time by the ringing of church bells. The bells marked the hours of prayer, but they also structured daily life more broadly, signaling when markets opened, when workers should begin and end their labor, and when town gates would close for the night.$q$,$q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$,$j$["Medieval markets stayed open only during hours of prayer.","Town gates in medieval Europe were closed throughout the day.","Mechanical clocks were invented to replace unreliable church bells.","Church bells helped organize many parts of daily life in medieval European towns."]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$The text's focus is that bells structured daily life broadly — markets, work, and town gates — not only prayer.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',2,$q$Ecologist Dana Okafor studied a lake where an invasive snail had spread. She hypothesized that the snail, which feeds on algae growing on rocks, was indirectly harming a native fish species whose young hide among that algae to avoid predators.$q$,$q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Okafor's hypothesis?$q$,$j$["Adult native fish occasionally eat the invasive snail.","The invasive snail is larger than most native snail species in the lake.","Survival of young native fish fell in areas where the snail had reduced algae cover on rocks.","Algae grows more quickly in summer than in winter."]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The hypothesis links fewer algae (from the snail) to harm for young fish that hide in it. A drop in young fish survival where algae declined directly supports that link.$q$,$j${"0":"This would suggest a benefit to the fish, not harm."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',2,$q$Some historians argue that the spread of inexpensive printed almanacs in the eighteenth century changed how ordinary farmers planned their year. Almanacs offered weather predictions, planting calendars, and advice, but historian Ruth Abernathy cautions that farmers' surviving diaries often record decisions that contradict their almanacs' recommendations, suggesting that ______$q$,$q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$,$j$["almanacs contained no information about planting.","almanacs were too expensive for most farmers to purchase.","farmers' diaries are unreliable records of the eighteenth century.","farmers did not necessarily follow almanac advice even when they owned almanacs."]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$If diaries show decisions contradicting almanac advice, the logical inference is that owning an almanac didn't mean following it.$q$,$j${"1":"The text calls the almanacs inexpensive."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000008','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',2,$q$Average monthly rainfall (millimeters)
City: January | April | July | October
Port Sela: 120 | 85 | 10 | 95
Marrow Bay: 40 | 60 | 110 | 70

A student claims that Port Sela has a dry summer while Marrow Bay has a wet summer.$q$,$q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the student's claim?$q$,$j$["Port Sela receives only 10 millimeters of rain in July, while Marrow Bay receives 110 millimeters.","Marrow Bay receives more rain in October than in April.","Both cities receive between 60 and 95 millimeters of rain in April and October.","Port Sela receives 120 millimeters of rain in January, while Marrow Bay receives 40 millimeters."]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$July is the summer month in the table. Port Sela's 10 mm versus Marrow Bay's 110 mm directly shows a dry versus wet summer.$q$,$j${"3":"January data describes winter, not summer."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000009','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',2,$q$The octopus has a remarkable ability to change color, yet most octopus species appear to be colorblind, possessing only one type of light-sensitive cell in their eyes. Some researchers have proposed that octopuses may detect color in an unusual way: light-sensitive proteins have been found in their skin, raising the possibility that ______$q$,$q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$,$j$["octopuses change color only when they are in complete darkness.","octopus skin proteins prevent the animals from changing color.","octopuses have more types of light-sensitive cells in their eyes than humans do.","octopuses sense some information about light through their skin rather than only through their eyes."]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$Light-sensitive proteins in the skin suggest the skin itself may detect light, which could explain color matching despite colorblind eyes.$q$,$j${"2":"This contradicts the text's statement that octopuses have only one type of light-sensitive cell."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',2,$q$Text 1
Early studies suggested that playing classical music to infants boosted their later performance on reasoning tasks. This idea became popular, and products promising to raise children's intelligence through music sold widely.

Text 2
Attempts to replicate the original findings have largely failed. Where effects appeared, they were small, short-lived, and explained by the music improving listeners' mood and alertness rather than by any lasting change in intelligence.$q$,$q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the popular idea described in Text 1?$q$,$j$["By claiming that music has no effect at all on mood or alertness","By arguing that the evidence doesn't support claims that music raises children's intelligence over the long term","By agreeing that classical music causes lasting increases in reasoning ability","By suggesting that products should use different styles of music"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Text 2 says replications failed and any effects were small, brief, and due to mood — undermining claims of lasting intelligence gains.$q$,$j${"0":"Text 2 actually credits music with improving mood and alertness."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',2,$q$Glaciologists measure the age of ice in Antarctic cores by counting annual layers, much like tree rings. In the deepest parts of a core, however, pressure thins and distorts the layers until they can no longer be distinguished. For these sections, researchers instead rely on chemical signatures, such as volcanic ash from eruptions with known dates.$q$,$q$According to the text, why do researchers use volcanic ash to date some parts of ice cores?$q$,$j$["Volcanic ash is found only near the surface of the ice.","Ice near the top of a core contains no annual layers.","Counting layers is more expensive than chemical analysis.","Annual layers in the deepest sections are too distorted to count."]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$The text says that deep layers become too thin and distorted to distinguish, so researchers turn to chemical markers like ash.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',2,$q$Survey of 400 residents about a proposed bike lane
Age group: Support | Oppose | Undecided
18–34: 72 | 18 | 10
35–54: 55 | 35 | 10
55 and older: 38 | 50 | 12
(Values are percents of each age group.)

A city planner notes that support for the bike lane declines with age, ______$q$,$q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$,$j$["rising from 18% opposition among the youngest group to 35% among those aged 35–54.","with 10% of residents aged 18–34 undecided.","although 50% of residents 55 and older oppose it.","falling from 72% among residents aged 18–34 to 38% among those 55 and older."]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$The claim is about support declining with age; the support percentages across age groups (72% → 55% → 38%) show that directly.$q$,$j${"0":"This describes opposition, not support."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',3,$q$Paleontologists long assumed that a group of large, long-necked dinosaurs browsed on tall trees, like modern giraffes. A team analyzing fossil neck vertebrae found that the joints allowed far more side-to-side movement than up-and-down movement. The team concluded that the dinosaurs more likely swept their necks horizontally to feed on low vegetation across a wide area.$q$,$q$Which finding, if true, would most strongly support the team's conclusion?$q$,$j$["These dinosaurs lived in regions that also contained very tall trees.","Fossilized stomach contents from these dinosaurs consist mainly of ferns and other low-growing plants.","The dinosaurs' teeth were similar in shape to those of other large plant-eaters.","Modern giraffes have neck vertebrae that allow little side-to-side movement."]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$If the dinosaurs mostly ate low-growing plants, that directly supports the claim that they fed on low vegetation rather than tall trees.$q$,$j${"0":"Tall trees being present doesn't show the dinosaurs ate from them — if anything, it fits the old view."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',3,$q$Economist Lena Varga studied towns that gained high-speed internet access in the early 2000s. She found that new small businesses opened at higher rates in those towns than in comparable towns without access. Varga cautions, however, that towns that obtained access earliest also tended to have growing populations, so ______$q$,$q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$,$j$["population growth, rather than internet access alone, may account for some of the increase in new businesses.","towns without internet access had shrinking populations.","internet access clearly caused all of the new businesses to open.","small businesses are unlikely to benefit from internet access."]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$Varga's caution identifies population growth as a possible confounding factor, so the logical completion is that it may explain part of the increase.$q$,$j${"2":"Her caution is exactly that this causal claim can't be made."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000015','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',3,$q$In many coastal wetlands, salt marsh grasses grow in distinct bands. Ecologists once explained this pattern entirely by tolerance to flooding: species that can survive long periods underwater grow closest to the sea. Later experiments showed that when the inland species were removed, the flood-tolerant species expanded upslope and thrived, indicating that ______$q$,$q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$,$j$["competition with inland species, not only flooding, limits where flood-tolerant species grow.","flood-tolerant species cannot survive away from the sea.","the banding pattern is caused by differences in soil color.","inland species are more tolerant of flooding than seaward species."]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$If flood-tolerant species thrive upslope once competitors are removed, their absence there was due to competition, not an inability to survive.$q$,$j${"1":"The experiment showed they did survive — and thrive — upslope."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000016','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',3,$q$Average daily screen time and sleep, by group (hours)
Group: Screen time | Sleep
A: 2 | 8.5
B: 4 | 8.1
C: 6 | 7.4
D: 8 | 6.9

A student writes that the data show screen time causes teenagers to sleep less.$q$,$q$Which choice best describes a weakness in the student's claim?$q$,$j$["The data show that groups with more screen time sleep more.","The data show an association between screen time and sleep but do not establish that one causes the other.","The data include only two variables, so no pattern can be identified.","Sleep of 6.9 hours is within the healthy range for teenagers."]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$The table shows that more screen time is associated with less sleep, but observational data like this can't establish causation.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000017','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',1,$q$The axolotl, a salamander native to lakes near Mexico City, can regrow lost limbs, parts of its heart, and even portions of its brain. Unlike most amphibians, it also retains its larval features, including feathery external gills, throughout its adult life.$q$,$q$According to the text, what is one way the axolotl differs from most amphibians?$q$,$j$["It is unable to regrow lost limbs.","It can live only in saltwater.","It keeps its larval features as an adult.","It lives in lakes far from Mexico City."]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The text says that, unlike most amphibians, the axolotl retains larval features throughout adulthood.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000018','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',2,$q$The architect of a new library chose to place its reading rooms along the north side of the building. Light from the north, she explained, is indirect and consistent throughout the day, so ______$q$,$q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$,$j$["the reading rooms would be warmer in the winter than other rooms.","the south side of the building would receive no light.","the library would need no electric lighting at night.","readers would have steady light without harsh glare or shifting shadows."]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$Indirect, consistent light logically leads to steady illumination without glare — the reason to put reading rooms there.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000019','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',2,$q$A team of biologists tracked the migration of monarch butterflies using tiny radio tags. Earlier studies had relied on observers spotting butterflies at fixed locations, which could reveal where monarchs passed but not the routes of individual insects. The radio tags allowed the team to follow single butterflies across hundreds of kilometers.$q$,$q$Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?$q$,$j$["To argue that monarch butterflies no longer migrate long distances","To explain how a new tracking method provided information that earlier methods could not","To describe the physical appearance of monarch butterflies","To criticize observers for making errors in earlier studies"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$The text contrasts earlier fixed-location observation with radio tags that could follow individual butterflies.$q$,$j${"3":"The text describes a limitation of the old method, not errors by observers."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000020','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',3,$q$Results of a fertilizer trial on tomato yield (kilograms per plot)
Fertilizer: Trial 1 | Trial 2 | Trial 3
None: 21 | 20 | 22
Type X: 29 | 30 | 28
Type Y: 24 | 31 | 19

A researcher concluded that Type X produced a more reliable improvement in yield than Type Y did.$q$,$q$Which choice best describes data from the table that support the researcher's conclusion?$q$,$j$["Type Y produced the single highest yield of any trial.","Plots with no fertilizer produced between 20 and 22 kilograms.","Type X and Type Y produced the same yield in Trial 2.","Type X increased yields by similar amounts in every trial, while Type Y's results varied widely and once fell below the no-fertilizer plot."]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$"Reliable" refers to consistency. Type X stayed at 28–30 kg every time; Type Y ranged from 19 to 31, falling below the 22 kg unfertilized plot in Trial 3.$q$,$j${"0":"One high result says nothing about consistency; Type Y's results actually varied widely."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000021','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',3,$q$Historian Marcus Bell argues that the rapid adoption of standardized time zones in the 1880s was driven less by public demand than by railroad companies, whose schedules were confused by the hundreds of local times used by different towns. Many towns, Bell notes, continued to keep their own local time for years after the railroads had adopted the new zones.$q$,$q$How does the information about towns keeping local time relate to Bell's argument?$q$,$j$["It explains why railroad schedules were never confusing.","It weakens the argument by showing that towns preferred standard time.","It shows that railroads opposed standardized time zones.","It supports the argument by suggesting that the public was not the main force behind standard time."]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$If many towns kept local time for years, the public wasn't pushing for standard time — consistent with Bell's view that railroads drove the change.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000022','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-info-ideas',1,$q$Mangrove forests grow along tropical coastlines, where their tangled roots slow incoming waves. By reducing the force of storm surges, mangroves help protect coastal communities from flooding and erosion.$q$,$q$According to the text, how do mangrove forests help coastal communities?$q$,$j$["They prevent tropical storms from forming.","Their roots slow waves, reducing flooding and erosion.","They provide fresh drinking water.","Their leaves are used to build homes."]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$The text says the roots slow waves and reduce storm surges, which protects against flooding and erosion.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000023','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',1,$q$The chef's new menu was deliberately ______: each dish used no more than five ingredients, allowing the quality of each one to stand out.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$,$j$["obsolete","unpredictable","extravagant","minimalist"]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$Using few ingredients so each stands out describes a minimalist approach.$q$,$j${"2":"Extravagant suggests excess — the opposite of five-ingredient dishes."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000024','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',1,$q$Although the scientist's early findings were met with doubt, later experiments by other laboratories ______ her results, and her conclusions became widely accepted.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$,$j$["corroborated","disregarded","fabricated","concealed"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$Her conclusions became accepted because other labs confirmed — corroborated — her results.$q$,$j${"1":"Disregarded results wouldn't lead to wide acceptance."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000025','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',1,$q$The documentary avoids taking sides; it presents the arguments of both supporters and critics of the dam in a ______ manner, leaving viewers to reach their own conclusions.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$,$j$["balanced","hostile","careless","secretive"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$Presenting both sides without taking a side is balanced.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000026','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',1,$q$The hiking trail was so ______ that even experienced climbers needed ropes to navigate its steepest sections.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$,$j$["leisurely","crowded","treacherous","scenic"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$Needing ropes even for experienced climbers indicates the trail was dangerous — treacherous.$q$,$j${"0":"A leisurely trail wouldn't require ropes."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000027','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',2,$q$Most nineteenth-century critics dismissed the painter's loose brushwork as unfinished. Today, however, that same quality is regarded as ______, since it anticipated the techniques that later artists would make central to modern painting.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$,$j$["imitative","innovative","careless","conventional"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$"However" signals a contrast with the critics' dismissal, and anticipating later techniques means the work was ahead of its time — innovative.$q$,$j${"3":"Conventional contradicts \"anticipated\" later techniques."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000028','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',2,$q$Unlike her earlier novels, which ranged across decades and continents, the author's latest book is notably ______, taking place over a single afternoon in one small apartment.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$,$j$["sprawling","repetitive","ambiguous","confined"]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$"Unlike" sets up a contrast with wide-ranging novels; a single afternoon in one apartment is confined.$q$,$j${"0":"Sprawling describes the earlier novels, not the new one."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000029','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',2,$q$The committee's report did not recommend a single solution. Instead, it ______ several possible approaches, describing the costs and benefits of each without endorsing any of them.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$,$j$["mandated","outlined","rejected","invented"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Describing approaches without endorsing them means the report outlined them.$q$,$j${"0":"Mandated means required — the opposite of not endorsing."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',2,$q$Sea otters often float on their backs while eating, using their chests as tables. __They have even been observed placing a flat rock on their chests and striking shellfish against it to crack them open.__ This behavior makes sea otters one of the few mammals other than primates known to use tools.$q$,$q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$,$j$["It provides a specific example of the behavior that the final sentence identifies as tool use.","It describes a behavior unique to young sea otters.","It explains why sea otters float on their backs.","It introduces a claim that the final sentence argues against."]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$The underlined sentence describes otters using a rock to crack shellfish — the example that the last sentence calls tool use.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',2,$q$For decades, astronomers believed that Pluto's surface was frozen and unchanging. When a spacecraft flew past Pluto in 2015, it revealed vast plains of nitrogen ice with no visible craters. __Because craters build up over time, the absence of craters suggested that the surface had been reshaped relatively recently.__$q$,$q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$,$j$["It restates the belief held by astronomers for decades.","It describes the instruments the spacecraft used.","It explains why an observation challenged the earlier belief about Pluto.","It introduces an unrelated detail about Pluto's orbit."]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The underlined sentence interprets the missing craters as evidence of recent change, which challenges the idea of an unchanging surface.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000032','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',2,$q$Some city planners argue that adding lanes to congested highways is the best way to reduce traffic. Yet studies of many cities have found that new lanes often fill with additional drivers within a few years — a pattern known as induced demand. As a result, wider highways frequently end up just as congested as before.$q$,$q$Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?$q$,$j$["To argue that cities should build more highways","To describe the history of a particular city's traffic problems","To present evidence that challenges a common approach to reducing traffic","To explain how highways are constructed"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The text introduces the add-lanes view and then presents studies showing induced demand undermines it.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000033','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',3,$q$Text 1
Some linguists argue that the rise of text messaging is eroding young people's writing skills, pointing to abbreviations and missing punctuation in casual messages.

Text 2
Research comparing students' formal essays with their text messages has found that most students switch easily between registers, writing carefully in essays even if they abbreviate heavily in texts. Some studies even found that frequent texters had slightly stronger spelling skills.$q$,$q$Based on the texts, how would the researchers in Text 2 most likely respond to the linguists in Text 1?$q$,$j$["They would agree that texting has caused a decline in formal writing skills.","They would claim that students no longer send text messages.","They would argue that abbreviations should be allowed in formal essays.","They would argue that habits in casual messages don't necessarily carry over into formal writing."]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$Text 2's research shows students switch registers and write carefully in essays, which counters the claim that texting habits damage writing.$q$,$j${"2":"Text 2 doesn't argue for changing essay standards."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000034','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',3,$q$The following text is adapted from an essay about public parks.
A city's great parks are often praised for their beauty, and rightly so. But to see them only as decorations is to miss their deeper function. A park is a kind of commons: a rare place where people of every income and background share the same ground, on equal terms, simply by showing up.$q$,$q$Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?$q$,$j$["To explain how parks are designed and maintained","To describe the features that make a park beautiful","To argue that public parks serve an important social role beyond their appearance","To criticize cities for spending too much money on parks"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The author grants that parks are beautiful but argues their "deeper function" is as a shared commons for everyone.$q$,$j${"1":"Beauty is acknowledged only to set up the main point."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000035','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',3,$q$The historian's account of the trade dispute is admirably thorough, drawing on letters, shipping records, and newspapers from both countries. Her conclusions, however, are more ______ than the evidence warrants: she presents as certain several claims that the documents only partly support.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$,$j$["exhaustive","definitive","tentative","modest"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Presenting partly supported claims as certain means her conclusions are more definitive than the evidence justifies.$q$,$j${"2":"Tentative conclusions would be appropriately cautious, not overstated.","3":"Modest is the opposite of overstating."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000036','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',3,$q$Biologist Amara Singh had expected that birds in noisy cities would simply sing louder than their rural counterparts. Her recordings showed something more ______: many urban birds had shifted their songs to a higher pitch, rising above the low rumble of traffic rather than trying to overpower it.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$,$j$["nuanced","simplistic","redundant","predictable"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$Shifting pitch rather than volume is a subtler, more complex response than she expected — more nuanced.$q$,$j${"3":"Singh's expectation was the simple prediction; the result was more complex."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000037','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',3,$q$In her memoir, the engineer recalls her first bridge design. __She had calculated every load with precision, yet when the model was tested, it swayed alarmingly in a moderate wind — a force she had not thought to consider.__ That failure, she writes, taught her that mastering formulas was only the beginning of understanding how structures behave in the world.$q$,$q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$,$j$["It presents an experience that leads to the lesson described in the final sentence.","It contrasts two different approaches to bridge design.","It argues that the engineer's calculations were incorrect.","It describes the engineer's most successful design."]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$The sentence describes the failed test, which the final sentence identifies as teaching her a lesson.$q$,$j${"2":"Her calculations were precise; she missed a force, not made an arithmetic error."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000038','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',1,$q$The new phone's battery life is ______: it lasts for two full days of heavy use without needing a charge.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$,$j$["impressive","temporary","inadequate","unknown"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$Lasting two days of heavy use is impressive.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000039','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',2,$q$The following text is from a short story.
Mr. Halloran kept the shop exactly as his father had left it. The brass bell above the door still rang when customers entered, the shelves still held jars labeled in his father's careful handwriting, and the ledger on the counter remained open to a page dated thirty years before.$q$,$q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$,$j$["It introduces a character's habit and then gives details that illustrate it.","It describes a problem and then presents a solution to it.","It compares two characters and explains their differences.","It recounts a sequence of events leading to a change in the shop."]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$The first sentence says he kept the shop unchanged; the rest gives examples — the bell, the jars, the ledger.$q$,$j${"3":"Nothing changes; the details emphasize that the shop has stayed the same."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000040','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',2,$q$Many people assume that the four-leaf clover is a separate species. In fact, four-leaf clovers are ordinary white clover plants with an uncommon variation, ______ by a combination of genetic and environmental factors.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$,$j$["prevented","replaced","caused","defined"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The variation results from — is caused by — genetic and environmental factors.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000041','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',3,$q$Text 1
Archaeologists have long thought that the large stone circles found across the region were built by a single culture over a short period.

Text 2
New dating of materials found beneath the stones indicates that construction took place in phases spread across nearly a thousand years. Differences in the stone-working techniques between phases suggest that more than one group may have contributed.$q$,$q$Based on the texts, the author of Text 2 would most likely view the belief described in Text 1 as$q$,$j$["fully confirmed by the new dating of materials.","inconsistent with new evidence about when and by whom the circles were built.","irrelevant to the study of stone circles.","correct about the timeline but wrong about the builders."]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Text 2's evidence contradicts both the single culture and the short period described in Text 1.$q$,$j${"3":"Text 2 challenges the timeline too — a thousand years is not a short period."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000042','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',2,$q$The speaker's argument was difficult to follow because she frequently ______ from her main point, telling long stories that seemed unrelated to the topic.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$,$j$["concluded","digressed","benefited","quoted"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Telling unrelated stories means she wandered — digressed — from her point.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000043','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',3,$q$Composer Clara Wieck Schumann was among the most celebrated pianists of the nineteenth century, performing across Europe for more than sixty years. __Her compositions, by contrast, received relatively little attention during her lifetime and were rarely performed for decades after her death.__ Only in recent years have orchestras and scholars begun to give her works the sustained attention they deserve.$q$,$q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$,$j$["It explains why Schumann stopped performing.","It argues that Schumann's performances were overrated.","It contrasts the reception of Schumann's compositions with the acclaim for her performing.","It provides evidence that Schumann's compositions were widely performed."]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$"By contrast" sets her overlooked compositions against her celebrated performing career.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000044','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-craft-structure',1,$q$The museum's new exhibit is ______; visitors can touch, build with, and rearrange nearly every object on display.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$,$j$["interactive","fragile","permanent","abstract"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$Visitors touching and rearranging objects describes an interactive exhibit.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000045','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-expression-ideas',1,$q$The city's first public library opened in 1902 with fewer than 2,000 books. ______ its collection had grown to more than 100,000 volumes.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$,$j$["For example,","Instead,","In contrast,","By 1950,"]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$The sentence moves forward in time from 1902; "By 1950," provides that time sequence.$q$,$j${"0":"The second sentence isn't an example of the first."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000046','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-expression-ideas',1,$q$Hummingbirds have the highest metabolism of any warm-blooded animal. ______ they must eat almost constantly, visiting hundreds of flowers each day.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$,$j$["However,","As a result,","Nevertheless,","Similarly,"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$Constant eating is a consequence of a high metabolism, so a cause-and-effect transition fits.$q$,$j${"0":"There's no contrast between the two sentences."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000047','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-expression-ideas',1,$q$Many deserts are hot during the day. ______ temperatures in some deserts can drop below freezing at night.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$,$j$["However,","For instance,","Therefore,","Likewise,"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$Freezing nights contrast with hot days, so a contrast transition is needed.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000048','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-expression-ideas',1,$q$The recipe calls for fresh basil. ______ dried basil can be used if fresh is unavailable, though the flavor will be milder.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$,$j$["Specifically,","In addition,","Consequently,","Alternatively,"]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$The sentence offers another option, so "Alternatively" fits.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000049','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-expression-ideas',2,$q$Engineers designing the new stadium wanted it to stay cool without air conditioning. They added a roof that reflects sunlight and openings that channel breezes through the seating areas. ______ the stadium uses far less energy than similar venues.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$,$j$["Thanks to these features,","Before then,","On the other hand,","Even so,"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$Using less energy results from the design features just described.$q$,$j${"3":"\"Even so\" implies the outcome happened despite the features."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000050','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-expression-ideas',2,$q$Early maps of the region showed a large inland lake. Later surveys, ______ found only a series of seasonal marshes that flooded after heavy rains.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$,$j$["in other words,","similarly,","however,","for example,"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$The later surveys contradict the early maps, so a contrast transition is needed.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000051','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-expression-ideas',2,$q$Octavia Butler's novels often explore power and survival. In Kindred, a modern woman is pulled back in time to the antebellum South. ______ in Parable of the Sower, a young woman struggles to survive in a collapsing society.$q$,$q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$,$j$["In contrast,","Instead,","Similarly,","As a result,"]$j$::jsonb,2,$q$Both novels illustrate the shared theme of power and survival, so "Similarly" fits.$q$,$j${"0":"Both novels share the theme; they aren't being contrasted."}$j$::jsonb,71,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
('5b020000-0000-4000-8000-000000000052','SAT',$q$Reading and Writing$q$,'sat-expression-ideas',2,$q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
