For years, the empty lot on Alder Street was a place that was full and crowded with weeds, broken glass, and an abandoned shopping cart. Most neighbors walked past it without a second glance, my aunt Rosa saw something different. She had grown up on a farm, and they imagined rows of tomatoes and beans where the trash now lay.

In the spring of 2019, Rosa organizes a meeting at the community center. Only six people came. Therefore, they left with a plan: they would ask the city for permission to use the lot, and they would clean it themselves.

