('5b030000-0002-4000-8000-000000000013','ACT',$q$English$q$,'act-eng-conventions',1,$q$Passage II — Libraries on Wheels
In the early twentieth century, many rural Americans lived miles from the nearest library. To reach them, some counties began sending libraries out on __wheels__. These "book wagons," later known as bookmobiles, were often converted carriages and, later, trucks lined with sturdy, wooden, shelves.

One of the earliest began operating in Washington County, Maryland, in 1905. A librarian, named Mary Lemist Titcomb thought of and came up with the idea of a horse-drawn wagon that would carry books directly to farms and villages. However, the wagon traveled routes through the countryside, stopping at general stores and farmhouses.

The service was popular, and families who had owned only a few books could suddenly borrow dozens each year. When the original wagon was destroyed in an accident, the county eventually replace it with a motorized truck.

Bookmobiles spread across the country over the following decades. Today, though their numbers have declined, they still serve rural areas, schools, and senior centers, reaching people who cannot easily travel to a library.$q$,$q$Which choice is best for the underlined portion?$q$,$j$["NO CHANGE","wheels's","wheel's","wheels'"]$j$::jsonb,0,$q$"Wheels" is a plural noun here, not a possessive, so no apostrophe is needed.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,42,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
