('5b030000-0003-4000-8000-000000000024','ACT',$q$English$q$,'act-eng-conventions',1,$q$Passage III — The Long Notes
I was eleven when I first picked up a cello. It was my __mothers'__, a dented instrument she had played in her high school orchestra, and it smelled faintly of rosin and old wood. My first attempts sounded like a door creaking open in a horror movie.

My teacher, Mr. Okafor, wouldn't never allow me to rush. For the first month, he had me play nothing but long notes that were slow and also long in their duration on open strings. Therefore, I wanted to play real music, not exercises, and I told him so. He smiled and said, "The music is in the long notes."

It wasn't until my first recital, two years later, that I understood what he meant. As I played a simple melody, I noticed that every note was steady, and full the the result of all those hours spent on open strings. Looking out at the audience, I felt for the first time that the cello and I were speaking the same language.

I still play every day. Before I practice anything else, I play long, slow notes, just as Mr. Okafor taught me — and each one remind me of my favorite composers.$q$,$q$Which choice is best for the underlined portion?$q$,$j$["NO CHANGE","mother's","mothers's","mothers"]$j$::jsonb,1,$q$The cello belongs to one mother, so the singular possessive "mother's" is correct.$q$,$j${}$j$::jsonb,42,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
