I was eleven when I first picked up a cello. It was my mothers', a dented instrument she had played in her high school orchestra, and it smelled faintly of rosin and old wood. My first attempts sounded like a door creaking open in a horror movie.

My teacher, Mr. Okafor, wouldn't never allow me to rush. For the first month, he had me play nothing but long notes that were slow and also long in their duration on open strings. __Therefore,__ I wanted to play real music, not exercises, and I told him so. He smiled and said, "The music is in the long notes."

It wasn't until my first recital, two years later, that I understood what he meant. As I played a simple melody, I noticed that every note was steady, and full the the result of all those hours spent on open strings. Looking out at the audience, I felt for the first time that the cello and I were speaking the same language.

I still play every day. Before I practice anything else, I play long, slow notes, just as Mr. Okafor taught me — and each one remind me of my favorite composers.$q$,$q$Which choice is best for the underlined portion?$q$,$j$["NO CHANGE","As a result,","In other words,","At first,"]$j$::jsonb,3,$q$The narrator's early impatience contrasts with the understanding that comes later, so "At first" sets up that shift. Nothing here is a result of the previous sentence.$q$,$j${"0":"Wanting to play real music isn't a consequence of playing long notes."}$j$::jsonb,42,'Original ScoreSpring practice content (AI-drafted; answer keys checked)','ai_generated','approved'),
